        "original_id": "44da97d1-efa4-5113-bd34-a7b844632afe",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Awki, Jacabamba, Andes, South America\n- Topics: Andean geography, glaciers in South America, Riograndia\n- Biological taxa: Riograndia\n- Concepts: mountain ranges, climatic regimes\n\nFacts:\n- The Andes, also called the Andean Mountains and in Spanish \"Cordillera de los Andes,\" are the longest continental mountain range in the world.\n- The Andes extend north to south through Venezuela, Colombia, Ecuador, Peru, Bolivia, Argentina, and Chile.\n- Glaciers in South America develop exclusively on the Andes.\n- Glaciers in South America are subject to the Andes climatic regimes of the Tropical Andes, Dry Andes, and Wet Andes.\n- Riograndia is a genus of tritheledontid cynodont from the Late Triassic of South America.\n- Awki lies southeast of Jacabamba.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "b99d3abc-e44e-5597-9da8-8c1bada62f55",
        "text": "Page: Auqui. Sentence: Awki lies southeast of Jacabamba.\nPage: Andes. Sentence: The Andes or Andean Mountains (Spanish: \"Cordillera de los Andes\" ) are the longest continental mountain range in the world.\nPage: Andes. Sentence: The Andes extend from north to south through seven South American countries: Venezuela, Colombia, Ecuador, Peru, Bolivia, Argentina and Chile.\nPage: List of glaciers in South America. Sentence: Glaciers in South America develop exclusively on the Andes and are subject to the Andes various climatic regimes namely the Tropical Andes, Dry Andes and the Wet Andes.\nPage: Riograndia. Sentence: Riograndia is a genus of tritheledontid cynodont from the Late Triassic of South America.",
